    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Given that \\(\\det \\mathbf{M} = -2\\), we need to find \\(\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4)\\).\n\nFirst, recall a fundamental property of determinants: the determinant of a matrix raised to a power is the determinant of the original matrix raised to that power. Mathematically, this is expressed as:\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{A}^n) = (\\det A)^n\n\\]\nIn our case, \\(A = \\mathbf{M}\\) and \\(n = 4\\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) = (\\det \\mathbf{M})^4\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\(\\det \\mathbf{M} = -2\\), we substitute this value into the equation:\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) = (-2)^4\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\((-2)^4\\):\n\\[\n(-2)^4 = (-2) \\times (-2) \\times (-2) \\times (-2) = 16\n\\]\n\nThus, the determinant of \\(\\mathbf{M}^4\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{16}\n\\]",
